LeetCode 102 — Binary Tree Level Order Traversal
Problem

Given the root of a binary tree, return its level-order traversal.

Level order means:

Visit nodes level by level from left to right.

Example
        3
       / \
      9   20
         /  \
        15   7

Output:

[[3], [9,20], [15,7]]

Approach 1: Brute Force — DFS + Level
Idea

Use DFS and keep track of the current level.

For every node:

If the list for that level doesn't exist, create it.
Add the current node to that level.
Recursively process left and right children.

Although DFS is used, we construct the result level by level.

Java
class Solution {

    public List<List<Integer>> levelOrder(TreeNode root) {

        List<List<Integer>> ans = new ArrayList<>();

        dfs(root, 0, ans);

        return ans;
    }

    private void dfs(TreeNode root, int level,
                      List<List<Integer>> ans) {

        if (root == null) {
            return;
        }

        if (ans.size() == level) {
            ans.add(new ArrayList<>());
        }

        ans.get(level).add(root.val);

        dfs(root.left, level + 1, ans);
        dfs(root.right, level + 1, ans);
    }
}

Dry Run

Tree:

        3
       / \
      9   20
         /  \
        15   7

DFS:

3 → level 0
9 → level 1
20 → level 1
15 → level 2
7 → level 2

Result:

[[3], [9,20], [15,7]]
Complexity
Time  = O(n)
Space = O(h)

h = tree height, excluding the output list.

Approach 2: Better — DFS with Height/Level

This is essentially the same DFS idea but can be thought of as explicitly building each level.

The important observation is:

level = distance from root

For example:

        3          level 0
       / \
      9   20       level 1
         /  \
        15   7     level 2

Java
class Solution {

    public List<List<Integer>> levelOrder(TreeNode root) {

        List<List<Integer>> ans = new ArrayList<>();

        if (root == null) {
            return ans;
        }

        int height = getHeight(root);

        for (int level = 0; level < height; level++) {

            List<Integer> current = new ArrayList<>();

            addLevel(root, level, current);

            ans.add(current);
        }

        return ans;
    }

    private int getHeight(TreeNode root) {

        if (root == null) {
            return 0;
        }

        return 1 + Math.max(
            getHeight(root.left),
            getHeight(root.right)
        );
    }

    private void addLevel(TreeNode root,
                           int level,
                           List<Integer> list) {

        if (root == null) {
            return;
        }

        if (level == 0) {
            list.add(root.val);
            return;
        }

        addLevel(root.left, level - 1, list);
        addLevel(root.right, level - 1, list);
    }
}

Complexity

Worst case:

Time = O(n²)
Space = O(h)

Why can time become O(n²)?

Because we repeatedly traverse parts of the tree for each level.

So this is not preferred.

Approach 3: BFS Using Queue ⭐ Most Optimal
Key Idea

Level Order Traversal naturally follows:

Top → Bottom
Left → Right

This is exactly what BFS (Breadth First Search) does.

Use a Queue.

Algorithm
Put root into queue.
Get current queue size.
That size represents the number of nodes in the current level.
Process exactly those nodes.
Add their children to the queue.
Repeat.

Java — Your Code

Your approach is the standard optimal solution. I've added proper generics to make it cleaner Java:

class Solution {

    public List<List<Integer>> levelOrder(TreeNode root) {

        List<List<Integer>> al = new ArrayList<>();

        if (root == null) {
            return al;
        }

        Queue<TreeNode> q = new LinkedList<>();

        q.add(root);

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {

            ArrayList<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();

            int size = q.size();

            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {

                TreeNode curr = q.poll();

                list.add(curr.val);

                if (curr.left != null) {
                    q.add(curr.left);
                }

                if (curr.right != null) {
                    q.add(curr.right);
                }
            }

            al.add(list);
        }

        return al;
    }
}

