Why int size = q.size() Is Important

This is one of the most important concepts in this problem.

Suppose:

Queue = [9, 20]

Then:

int size = q.size();

gives:

size = 2

So we process exactly these two nodes.

While processing them, their children are added:

9  → children
20 → children

Queue = [15,7]

But we don't process 15 and 7 in the same level, because our loop is:

for (int i = 0; i < size; i++)

where size was captured before processing the level.

This separates the tree into levels.

