Complexity

For BFS:

Time = O(n)

Every node is inserted and removed from the queue exactly once.

Auxiliary space:

O(w)

where w is the maximum width of the tree.

Worst case:

O(n)
Comparison of All Approaches
Approach	Technique	Time	Extra Space
Brute Force	DFS + level	O(n)	O(h)
Better	Height + DFS each level	O(n²) worst	O(h)
Most Optimal ⭐	BFS + Queue	O(n)	O(w)
Why BFS Is Preferred?

The problem asks:

Level 0
Level 1
Level 2
Level 3
...

BFS naturally processes:

Level 0 → Level 1 → Level 2 → ...

DFS instead goes:

Root → Deep Left → Backtrack → ...

Therefore:

Level Order Traversal → Think BFS → Think Queue

Important Pattern
Binary Tree
     ↓
Level by Level
     ↓
BFS
     ↓
Queue
Remember
int size = q.size();

is the key line.

It tells us:

How many nodes belong to the current level?

Then:

for (int i = 0; i < size; i++)

processes only that level.

Interview Shortcut ⭐

For any problem saying:

Level order
Level by level
Nodes at each depth
Minimum depth
Zigzag level order
Right/left view by levels

Immediately think:

BFS + Queue

For LeetCode 102, the cleanest expected solution is BFS with a queue, giving O(n) time.
